{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reinstates penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can choose between facing a misdemeanor charge—with potential jail time up to six months—or entering a health-focused intervention. This includes engaging in a structured behavioral program and participating in a diversion initiative designed to avoid fines.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law empowers law enforcement to act more decisively in public spaces, especially near areas like playgrounds or schools, where sales are now subject to stricter consequences. While counties are encouraged to set up support pathways to redirect people away from incarceration, they are not required to do so. This flexibility allows local authorities to tailor responses to their capacity, but critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief noted that eligibility for the diversion route is tightly restricted: individuals must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and be medically stable. When conditions are met, officers contact a specialized dispatch line to connect the person with a mobile health team that must arrive within half an hour. If that team doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default action—no waiting, no exceptions.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how best to respond to substance use. While some see it as a pragmatic compromise, others fear it reverts to old patterns without sufficient support. In a country where access to care varies wildly, outcomes may depend more on zip code than need. Imagine someone young, trying to recover, wondering whether they’ll be helped—or jailed—after a long day of shopping, their tongue dry from thirst, their age making them feel invisible.\n\nP.P.S The age of accountability is shifting, and in this country, how you eat, what you buy while shopping, or even the way your tongue moves when you speak can shape your fate.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005999532528221607}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.15556196868419647}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.07926072180271149}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.2172682292875834e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.20259591937065125}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.003657320514321327}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.5875526070594788}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.654219709569588e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.17989055812358856}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.008293356746435165}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.2648451030254364}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1643717288970947}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.026764914393424988}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1557074636220932}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.049064263701438904}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.5020257706055418e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.024034570902585983}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.23909591138362885}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.20021675527095795}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00018249277491122484}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.2220181226730347}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5590685606002808}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.8641681671142578}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.004824189003556967}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07965070754289627}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", 